WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.840
It goes back to the basic idea of
why are we studying a language?

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.420
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today
I wanna talk about what is the most

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.550
important thing in language learning?

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.350
The most important thing
in language learning.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.980
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.600
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.990
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.330
So what's important in language learning?

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.940
The answer is quite simple.

00:00:27.825 --> 00:00:33.195
What is important in language is whatever
is important to you, the learner.

00:00:33.885 --> 00:00:34.335
All right.

00:00:35.114 --> 00:00:39.825
And I'll explain what I mean by that
you'll remember, or those of you who

00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:44.055
follow me will remember the three keys
to language earning, which I often refer

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.765
to something that I heard at a language
teachers conference in San Diego many

00:00:48.765 --> 00:00:54.945
years ago, and a very experienced person
with many years of teaching told me that

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.680
there are only three things that matter
in language learning: the attitude of the

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.330
learner, the time spent with the language,
not reading grammar in your native

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:07.020
language but actually with the language
listening, reading, speaking, writing.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.960
And the third thing is
the ability to notice.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:14.280
And that is a little more difficult
to understand, but if we do research

00:01:14.285 --> 00:01:20.460
on things that we notice in life, we
notice things that are important to us.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.800
And so the importance of things becomes
a big factor in language acquisition.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.850
And I, I thought of doing this
video because I noticed that I was

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.590
listening to a mini story in Persian
for the, I don't know, 30th time and

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.585
I noticed something about verbs in
or particular, a particular verb in

00:01:42.585 --> 00:01:44.325
Persian that I hadn't noticed before.

00:01:45.255 --> 00:01:46.304
Why did I notice it?

00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:51.075
I didn't deliberately sort of plan
to notice that, but probably in the

00:01:51.075 --> 00:01:56.235
sort of evolution of my learning
of Persian, I'm now sort of open to

00:01:56.235 --> 00:02:00.914
noticing something which has now become
important to me because I noticed

00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:06.045
that I'm struggling with certain, you
know, patterns of verbs in Persian.

00:02:07.245 --> 00:02:09.914
And so then I noticed this
thing in, in this story.

00:02:10.815 --> 00:02:13.665
It's not that I set out, you know,
I'm gonna listen to this story

00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:17.535
and I'm gonna deliberately notice
the verbs or something like that.

00:02:17.535 --> 00:02:23.085
No, it's just that I was ready to
notice this particular verb and

00:02:23.085 --> 00:02:26.925
structure and having noticed it there.

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:28.545
And I'll probably notice it again.

00:02:28.545 --> 00:02:31.635
I think we've all had the experience
that once you notice something

00:02:31.665 --> 00:02:34.600
somewhere, you'll notice in other places.

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:40.209
And eventually then hopefully that
then becomes a pattern that I will

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:43.900
recognize and that I'll be able to
use, but it begins with the idea

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.640
that this now is important for me.

00:02:46.125 --> 00:02:49.995
Uh, you know, I remember when I was
learning Chinese characters and of

00:02:49.995 --> 00:02:53.955
course teachers like to teach you
things that they think are important.

00:02:54.135 --> 00:02:56.085
So the teacher decides what's important.

00:02:56.355 --> 00:03:00.375
So they taught me that the Chinese
characters have these radicals,

00:03:00.375 --> 00:03:04.785
these components that represent
either meaning or in many cases,

00:03:04.785 --> 00:03:09.705
the sound of the character, but it
made no, you know, impression on me.

00:03:09.705 --> 00:03:11.055
I couldn't really recognize them.

00:03:11.055 --> 00:03:13.605
It didn't really help
me learn the characters.

00:03:13.605 --> 00:03:16.905
I still felt that I had to learn
each character individually.

00:03:17.325 --> 00:03:22.995
Much, much later I began to see
the importance of these radicals or

00:03:22.995 --> 00:03:25.605
components in the Chinese charact.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:31.769
It's like, uh, you know, the teacher,
again, I've, I've mentioned this before,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.609
people who, um, write, uh, textbooks
for languages, feel that they, they

00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:45.750
wanna start with in, in some, in many
languages, relatives, you know, your uncle

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:49.049
on your mother's side, on your father's
side, your older sister, your younger

00:03:49.049 --> 00:03:51.540
sister, they all have different names.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.000
And so people want to teach
this to you right up front.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:58.335
And I always find that this is like
of no interest to me at this point.

00:03:58.335 --> 00:03:59.535
It's not important to me.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.045
There's so many other things that
I need to learn about the language

00:04:03.045 --> 00:04:04.755
that this simply is not important.

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:08.174
Uh, textbooks like to
introduce festivals to you.

00:04:08.625 --> 00:04:12.375
Again, it may be important to some
beginners, not important to me.

00:04:12.644 --> 00:04:16.245
At a later stage in my learning
I will find those things very

00:04:16.245 --> 00:04:18.765
interesting and therefore important.

00:04:19.274 --> 00:04:23.534
Um, you know, some textbooks like
to teach you all the colors at once.

00:04:23.685 --> 00:04:27.675
It's not important to me and
therefore I can't learn them when a

00:04:27.675 --> 00:04:29.565
particular color becomes important.

00:04:29.865 --> 00:04:31.815
I will notice it and I will learn it.

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:36.375
Um, you know, I was thinking too, it's
a bit like, uh, when we drive a car,

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:43.650
uh, if we approach an intersection,
um, I now as a driver with many, many

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:48.960
years of experience, I'm looking for
cars that might be coming from the

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.850
right hand side or the left hand side.

00:04:50.855 --> 00:04:54.510
Maybe they aren't, maybe they're
gonna run the intersection.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:00.570
Uh I'll notice, for example, if I'm
gonna turn right on a red light,

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:04.515
which I'm allowed to do, uh, are
there pedestrians crossing or

00:05:04.515 --> 00:05:06.615
attempting to cross the intersection?

00:05:06.915 --> 00:05:10.455
There's so many things that I naturally
notice that are important to me.

00:05:11.895 --> 00:05:16.995
At an early stage as a driver, when I'm
struggling with my manual gear shift or

00:05:17.295 --> 00:05:21.675
other things in the car that are more
important to me, I don't notice these

00:05:21.675 --> 00:05:27.285
things, or I might notice from the car
that there's a new restaurant, that's

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:31.065
just opened up in my, uh, area of town.

00:05:31.515 --> 00:05:35.280
And I'll notice a restaurant, but I
won't notice, for example, a nail salon,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.760
because I'm not interested in nail
salons, but I'm interested in restaurants.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:40.410
Restaurants are important to me.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.520
It goes back to the basic idea
of why are we studying a language

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.080
if learning that language is not
important to you, you won't learn.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:54.705
Uh, if the time you spend trying
to learn that language is not an

00:05:54.705 --> 00:05:56.955
important use of time, you won't learn.

00:05:57.405 --> 00:06:02.145
Uh, the more you can seek out a
content of interest content that

00:06:02.145 --> 00:06:05.985
is important to you to learn from
the better you're gonna learn.

00:06:06.495 --> 00:06:12.195
Um, if there are issues of grammar that
are important to you, you so important

00:06:12.195 --> 00:06:15.735
that you actually Google for them, or
look through a grammar book and look them.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:20.250
Uh, at a time when they are important to
you, you're more likely to learn them.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:24.000
If the teacher decides, okay, class
today, we're gonna learn the subjunctive

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:28.710
and you are struggling with so many
other things in the language that this

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.099
explanation of the subjunctive makes
no impression on you, because you're

00:06:32.099 --> 00:06:34.860
not ready to receive that information.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.960
You don't have other relevant information
or knowledge to make it more meaningful.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.280
It's not important to you.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.515
So there are so many ways in which
what's important in language learning

00:06:48.914 --> 00:06:54.300
is what's important to us, whether it
be in terms of the, the language content

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.740
that we learn from, or the aspects of
the language that we wanna learn about,

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:05.580
or simply the fact that a natural
process of, you know, having more and

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:11.640
more pieces of the jigsaw puzzle of that
language have been put on the board.

00:07:11.995 --> 00:07:14.935
And so now you are able to
focus on the missing pieces.

00:07:14.935 --> 00:07:16.974
These missing pieces are
now important to you.

00:07:17.485 --> 00:07:23.635
So, uh, I think the ability to, to, to
notice, uh, because we notice things that

00:07:23.635 --> 00:07:30.120
are important to us, it's it's in many
ways, a subconscious uh, process, but

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.430
at the same time, you have to be open.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:33.090
You ha...

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:39.690
you're not gonna notice things if you're
not open, uh, open to new, um, sights and

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:44.940
sounds, open to new ways of pronouncing
things open to new ways of saying things.

00:07:44.969 --> 00:07:48.990
If you are open to these things and
the language is important to you,

00:07:49.169 --> 00:07:54.270
gradually more and more aspects of
that language will become important.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.340
You will acquire, initially passively,
and then eventually you'll be able

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.890
to use them, uh, when speaking.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:08.400
So, uh, I'll leave you with a video
that I did about, um, you know, the

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:14.370
noticing hypothesis, which, uh, is
part of the sort of linguistics world

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:20.909
explaining how we learn and also an
old video on the importance of choosing

00:08:20.909 --> 00:08:22.530
content of interest to learn from.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.450
So thank you for listening.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:25.530
Bye for now.

